WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.806
Sockets are resources with lifecycles.

2
00:00:01.806 --> 00:00:06.141
They are created, configured, connected or accepted, used, shut down, and closed.

3
00:00:06.141 --> 00:00:10.114
In C++, that lifecycle should be represented clearly in ownership types.

4
00:00:10.114 --> 00:00:19.867
A socket handle that is passed around as an unadorned integer or platform handle is easy to leak, close twice, or keep alive longer than the protocol

5
00:00:19.867 --> 00:00:20.589
state allows.

6
00:00:20.589 --> 00:00:22.756
RAII wrappers are a strong default.

7
00:00:22.756 --> 00:00:29.619
The object that owns a descriptor or handle closes it when the object leaves scope, including on error paths.

8
00:00:29.619 --> 00:00:37.205
That does not remove the need for protocol-level shutdown, but it prevents cleanup from depending on scattered manual close calls.

9
00:00:37.205 --> 00:00:43.707
Move-only ownership can make it clear which part of the program is responsible for a live connection.

10
00:00:43.707 --> 00:00:46.596
Cross-platform code needs to respect platform differences.

11
00:00:46.596 --> 00:00:53.821
POSIX descriptors and Windows socket handles have different types and setup rules, and Windows networking may require library initialization and

12
00:00:53.821 --> 00:00:54.182
cleanup.

13
00:00:54.182 --> 00:00:59.961
These details should be hidden behind a small, tested abstraction rather than repeated throughout business logic.

14
00:00:59.961 --> 00:01:03.212
The abstraction should make invalid or closed states explicit.

15
00:01:03.212 --> 00:01:08.630
Connection lifecycles also include accept loops, cancellation, normal completion, protocol errors, authentication failures, and shutdown.

16
00:01:08.630 --> 00:01:17.299
A service should know how to stop accepting new connections, how to finish or cancel current work, and when a connection should be closed.

17
00:01:17.299 --> 00:01:23.440
Predictable cleanup protects availability because it keeps resource use understandable even when clients disconnect or behave unexpectedly.
